1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,185
So there's silica up
on the upper right.

2
00:00:02,185 --> 00:00:03,219
Can you see that?

3
00:00:03,219 --> 00:00:07,457
So fused silica, that's
basically almost quartz.

4
00:00:07,457 --> 00:00:09,292
That's basically quartz.

5
00:00:09,292 --> 00:00:13,830
So you are really close
to making a nice crystal,

6
00:00:13,830 --> 00:00:18,802
but notice how high of viscosity
you have versus temperature.

7
00:00:18,802 --> 00:00:21,838
Notice that you're at this very,
very high value for viscosity.

8
00:00:21,838 --> 00:00:24,374
It's a really thick material.

9
00:00:24,374 --> 00:00:26,342
And people care a
lot about viscosity

10
00:00:26,342 --> 00:00:27,410
when they work with glass.

11
00:00:27,410 --> 00:00:27,911
Why?

12
00:00:27,911 --> 00:00:31,981
Because you need to
know, for example,

13
00:00:31,981 --> 00:00:34,217
is the viscosity at a point
where I can work with it?

14
00:00:34,217 --> 00:00:35,418
Can I shape it?

15
00:00:35,418 --> 00:00:36,619
That's the bottom.

16
00:00:36,619 --> 00:00:38,655
That's the region that
says working range.

17
00:00:38,655 --> 00:00:39,823
I can still shape it.

18
00:00:39,823 --> 00:00:40,690
I can make a window.

19
00:00:43,426 --> 00:00:45,628
There it flows under
its own weight.

20
00:00:45,628 --> 00:00:48,965
So now I can shape it and
it will lose the shape.

21
00:00:48,965 --> 00:00:52,168
That's kind of important to know
if I'm making a glass animal.

22
00:00:54,838 --> 00:00:58,174
There its stress can
still be relieved.

23
00:00:58,174 --> 00:00:59,776
Remaining stress can
still be relieved,

24
00:00:59,776 --> 00:01:01,611
and up there you
can rapidly cool it

25
00:01:01,611 --> 00:01:03,479
without introducing new defects.

26
00:01:03,479 --> 00:01:06,316
But look at the tradeoff
between the temperature

27
00:01:06,316 --> 00:01:09,652
that you have to go to and the
viscosity and the viscosity

28
00:01:09,652 --> 00:01:10,620
that you have.

29
00:01:10,620 --> 00:01:13,890
So if you go now from
this almost quartz

30
00:01:13,890 --> 00:01:15,124
all the way down--

31
00:01:15,124 --> 00:01:18,127
OK, there you take a little
bit of stuff and you put it in,

32
00:01:18,127 --> 00:01:19,963
but it's still 96% silica.

33
00:01:19,963 --> 00:01:21,698
And now you go to
boiled silicate.

34
00:01:21,698 --> 00:01:27,103
Remember, it's like 75%, 80%
silica plus other additives.

35
00:01:27,103 --> 00:01:30,340
And look at how much you've
changed the temperature.

36
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:32,242
So I can now work
with this material

37
00:01:32,242 --> 00:01:37,780
below 1,000 C. That has huge
consequences for industry,

38
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:40,817
for actually making
different things out

39
00:01:40,817 --> 00:01:43,953
of glass, which we
now do regularly.

40
00:01:43,953 --> 00:01:46,222
And the Romans did it too,
but they didn't know why.

41
00:01:46,222 --> 00:01:48,458
They said, well, if you add
a little bit of this dirt,

42
00:01:48,458 --> 00:01:49,626
something different happens.

43
00:01:49,626 --> 00:01:51,628
Let's try a little
bit of that one.

44
00:01:51,628 --> 00:01:52,562
Now we know.

45
00:01:52,562 --> 00:01:54,063
We know why.
